==English==

===Etymology===
{{etydate|1440-1450's}}, in {{w|Middle English}}; {{bor+|en|la|satiātus|nocap=1}}, {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|la|satiō||to fill full, satiate, satisfy}} (see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}} and {{af|en|-ate|id1=adjective|pos1=adjective-forming suffix}}), from {{m|la|satis||sufficient}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈseɪʃɪeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-satiate.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fill]] to [[satisfaction]]; to [[satisfy]].
#: {{ux|en|Nothing seemed to '''satiate''' her desire for knowledge.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[satisfy]] to [[excess]]. To fill to [[satiety]].

====Usage notes====
Used interchangeably with, and more common than, {{m|en|sate}}.<ref>“[http://blog.oup.com/2011/11/nov-gleanings/ Monthly Gleanings: November 2011]: ''Sate'' versus ''satiated''.”, ''[http://blog.oup.com/ OUPblog]''</ref>

